course: religion-ethics
lesson: 8
title: Ruth, and David and Goliath
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ruth was a young widow from Moab, a foreign land, who would not leave her mother-in-law Naomi: «Where you go I will go, and your people will be my people».
explain: In Israel Ruth gleaned, gathering the leftover grain in the field of Boaz, as God's law let the poor do; Boaz protected her, gave her extra grain and later married her, and Ruth became the great-grandmother of King David.
explain: Years later the prophet Samuel anointed the youngest shepherd boy, David, as the future king, because «the Lord looks at the heart»; then the giant Goliath frightened the whole army, but David went out with a sling and five smooth stones and won with the first one.
explain: David's best friend was King Saul's son Jonathan, who protected him from Saul's jealousy, and when David could have killed Saul, who was hunting him, he refused.
explain: For a child today these stories mean: welcome the newcomer, be loyal to friends when it costs you, use the gifts you have instead of copying others, and do not take revenge even when you could.
fact: Ruth, a widow from Moab, stayed with her mother-in-law Naomi, saying «Where you go I will go».
fact: Ruth was not an Israelite, yet she was welcomed and became the great-grandmother of King David.
fact: Gleaning meant gathering grain left at the edges of a field; God's law let the poor and foreigners do it.
fact: Boaz protected Ruth, left extra grain for her and later married her.
fact: God sent the prophet Samuel to anoint the youngest son of Jesse, the shepherd boy David, as the future king.
fact: Samuel was told: «People look at the outward appearance, but the Lord looks at the heart».
fact: Goliath was a giant Philistine warrior who challenged Israel's army to send one man to fight him, and everyone was afraid.
fact: David refused King Saul's armour and took his sling and five smooth stones, trusting God.
fact: David's first stone struck Goliath and he fell; «David and Goliath» now means a win by the underdog.
fact: Jonathan, King Saul's son, became David's best friend and protected him from Saul's jealousy.
fact: David had the chance to kill King Saul, who was hunting him, but refused to harm him.
fact: Christians differ on whether every detail of these books is exact history; the lessons are agreed, the details are disputed.
exercise: What did Ruth say to Naomi when told to go home? | Where you go I will go | I must go back to my own people | Let us both return to Moab | I will visit you every summer
exercise: Where did Ruth come from? | Moab, a land outside Israel | Jerusalem, the holy city | Egypt, where Moses was born | Bethlehem, where Naomi lived
exercise: Whose great-grandmother did Ruth become? | King David's | Moses' | Abraham's | King Solomon's
exercise: What does Ruth's story teach about foreigners? | God's family includes outsiders, so newcomers should be welcomed | Foreigners should always go back home | Only people born in Israel mattered | Foreigners must change their names
exercise: What was gleaning? | Gathering leftover grain at the edges of a field | Selling all the grain at the market for a good price | Planting seeds in spring | Grinding wheat into flour
exercise: Why did God's law allow gleaning? | So that the poor and foreigners had food | So that the farmers could work a bit less | So that birds would not eat all of the grain | So that the kings could collect more taxes
exercise: How did Boaz show real kindness to Ruth? | He protected her and left extra grain for her | He paid her some money to leave his field and go home | He told her to work harder | He sent her back to Moab
exercise: Which son of Jesse did Samuel anoint? | David, the youngest | Eliab, the tallest | The oldest son | The strongest soldier
exercise: What did God tell Samuel about choosing a king? | The Lord looks at the heart, not the outward appearance | Choose the tallest and strongest son | Choose the son with the most sheep | Let the people vote for the best
exercise: Who was Goliath? | A giant Philistine warrior | A king of Israel before Saul | A prophet from the land of Moab | Jonathan's older brother, a soldier
exercise: Why was the whole army afraid? | Goliath was huge and challenged them to send a fighter | Goliath had a thousand chariots and ten thousand horsemen | The king had run away | They had no food left
exercise: What did David take to fight Goliath? | A sling and five smooth stones | Saul's heavy armour and his sword | A long spear and a bronze shield | A bow and a quiver of sharp arrows
exercise: Why did David refuse Saul's armour? | He used the gifts he had and trusted God | The armour was made of gold | He wanted to look braver than everyone else in the army | Saul refused to lend it
exercise: How many stones did David need to bring Goliath down? | One | Five | Three | Two
exercise: Who was David's best friend? | Jonathan, son of King Saul | Goliath's brother from Gath | The prophet Samuel, who anointed him | Boaz the farmer from Bethlehem
exercise: What did David do when he could have killed King Saul? | He refused to harm him | He took the throne at once | He called Goliath's army | He ran away to Egypt
exercise: What does «a David and Goliath match» mean today? | A contest where the small side faces the big one | A match between two giants of exactly the same size | A game with stones | A fight between friends
test: my friend: «ruth was an israelite princess who married david». fix it pls | Ruth was a foreign widow from Moab who married Boaz and was David's great-grandmother | Ruth was David's sister from Jerusalem | Ruth was a queen of Egypt who married Saul | Ruth was Goliath's mother from Philistia
test: A new kid from Syria joins the class and someone says «go back where you came from». What does Ruth's story say? | Newcomers can belong, as Ruth the foreigner did | Foreigners should stay with their own people, in their own land | It says nothing about newcomers | Only rich foreigners should be welcomed
test: «why should the farm leave food on the edge, that's their money innit». What is the idea of gleaning? | Leaving something for people in need is built-in fairness | Farmers were too lazy to finish | The edges were full of bad grain that nobody wanted to eat anyway | It was a trick to catch thieves
test: little bro: «david beat goliath cos he was secretly the biggest guy in the army». right? | No, he was a young shepherd who trusted God and used his sling | Yes, he was taller than Goliath and the strongest man in Israel | Yes, he wore the king's armour | No, Jonathan fought Goliath for him
test: kids pick teams: «not him, he's tiny and rubbish lol». Which line answers this? | People look at the outward appearance, but the Lord looks at the heart | Where you go I will go | Let my people go | Only the strongest should be chosen
test: «Saul tried to kill him, David should've got him back, easy». What did David actually do? | He spared Saul when he had the chance | He killed Saul with a stone from his sling | He told Jonathan to do it for him in secret | He hid in a cave until Saul died of old age
test: A classmate with no religion asks: «why was Jonathan such a good friend?» | He protected David from his own father's jealousy, even at a cost | He gave David his father's crown at once and made him king that same day | He fought Goliath for David | He was paid by Samuel to help
test: «the giant was just a big man lol, why was everyone so scared?» What does the story show? | Fear can freeze a whole crowd until one person trusts and acts | Goliath had magic powers | The soldiers were all asleep | The king had banned fighting
test: My mate wants to borrow my dad's huge football boots for the match. What would David's story say? | Use what fits you and the gifts you have | Always copy whatever the strongest player on the team does | Big boots always win games | Never play against bigger kids
test: A judge asks: «Was Ruth's choice only loyalty, or also courage?» | Both: she left her own land and people for a hard, unknown life | Only loyalty, it cost her nothing | Only courage, she did not love Naomi | Neither, she was told to go
test: A judge follows up: «Is every detail of Ruth and David agreed to be exact history?» | No, Christians differ on the details, though the lessons are agreed | Yes, every Christian agrees on every detail | Yes, there are photographs of Goliath | No, because Ruth never existed in any version of the Bible story at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which, according to the First Book of Samuel, the youngest son of Jesse was anointed. | The Lord regards the heart rather than the outward appearance | He was the eldest and the tallest of the sons, as was the custom | He had defeated Goliath previously | He was the son of King Saul
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice designated «gleaning»? | The gathering, by the poor, of grain remaining at the margins of a field | The sale of surplus grain by the landowner | The sowing of seed prior to the rains | The payment of a tithe of grain to the priests of the Temple at Jerusalem
exam: State the relationship of Ruth the Moabitess to King David. | Great-grandmother | Paternal grandmother | Maternal grandmother | Great-great-grandmother
exam: Select the response consistent with the established account concerning the conduct of David towards King Saul. | Having opportunity to slay Saul, David declined to do him harm | David slew Saul in single combat | David delivered Saul to the Philistines | David conspired with Jonathan to depose Saul
